Memories...

Hunt the Wumpus was indeed of the TRS-80 era.  It was a text game, played on
the vertices of a dodecahedron (seriously!).  You wandered from cavern to cavern
(each located on a vertex of the dodecahedron).  There was one wumpus, two
bottomless pits [death], and two caverns full of "super"-bats [random
teleport].  Walking into the wumpus' room (or shooting at him with your bow)
woke him up, and he'd move (25% to each adjoining room, 25% stay); if you were
at his location, he'd then eat you.

You could tell what was at the locations immediately adjoining your current
one by the use of informative messages ("I smell a wumpus!", "I feel a draft!"
and "I hear squeaking!").  We know that a wumpus had sucker feet and was
extremely heavy (and hungry), because the pits and bats didn't affect him.

The whole thing could probably be coded in two days
in C by an undergraduate nowadays, but in its day it was leading edge.

And no, there is no wumpus in AD&D, unless you have a warped DM.

